 date="2026-10-09T18:16:22Z"
 content="""
The attacker could also document that the user ought to `git-annex
enableremote` the remote, and so avoid any autoenable specific security
guardrails.

That suggests that enableremote needs the same kind of security guardrails.
That could be the same workflow with `git-annex verifyremote` needed after
enableremote. Or if could be built in to it, and rely on the user reading
to review it for safety:

	> git-annex enableremote foo
	enableremote foo
		Enabling a special remote with this configuration:
	                type=S3 bucket=foo
		If this is not the remote repository you expected
		to be using, run: git-annex verifyremote foo --unsafe
"""]]
